  "classes": [
    {
      "id": "guard",
      "name": "Guard",
      "baseStats": {
        "health": 7,
        "movement": 3,
        "strength": 4,
        "defense": 1,
        "actionPoints": 2
      },
      "allowedWeaponTypeIds": ["blade"],
      "notes": "Two action points, so a picket that steps off its tile can still swing. One would make the walk the whole turn, and a demo whose first lesson is that moving forfeits the strike teaches the wrong game. Seven health for the reason the heron rider has seven: the two classes are deliberately the same stat line, so the board turns on position and on who opens rather than on a number one side was handed."
    },
    {
      "id": "heron_rider",
      "name": "Heron Rider",
      "baseStats": {
        "health": 7,
        "movement": 3,
        "strength": 4,
        "defense": 1,
        "actionPoints": 2
      },
      "allowedWeaponTypeIds": ["blade"],
      "traversal": {
        "flying": true
      },
      "notes": "The Dawn Guard ride herons. The bridge is the only way over the water for anyone on foot — which is what the River Watch is standing on — and the heron goes over it instead, so the crossing is a choice rather than a corridor. Two action points, so the crossing and the strike are one turn: a rider who had to spend the turn arriving would make the flight worth nothing. Seven health, matching the picket's, because a blow is three: four strength against one defence, the same either way, and a counter is free. Whoever opens an exchange takes three from the counter and three more on the other side's turn, so four health would make every meeting one exchange long and hand it to whoever swung second: the rider who flew across and struck would be dead before its own second blow. Seven buys three exchanges instead of one, which is what lets flying across and striking be the move that wins rather than the move that loses."
    },
    {
      "id": "relay_rogue",
      "name": "Relay Rogue",
      "baseStats": {
